 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Channels",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The drivers take TTL/CMOS levels in and put EIA/TIA-232-F levels out; the receivers do the reverse, with 5 V TTL/CMOS outputs. One part covers the common 9-pin RS-232 port between data terminal equipment and data communications equipment, at data rates up to 120 kbit/s."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supplies",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The drivers run between VDD (7.5 V to 15 V) and VSS (-15 V to -7.5 V); the receivers run from VCC (4.5 V to 5.5 V). The driver output swing follows the VDD and VSS rails: about ±10 V at ±12 V supplies, ±7.3 V at ±9 V and ±4.6 V at ±6 V into 3 kΩ at 25 °C. VDD current is higher with the driver inputs high (outputs low) than with them low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Driver inputs and outputs",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "A driver input is high above 1.9 V and low below 0.8 V; the driver inverts, so a high input gives a negative output. Output current into a short to ground is limited to 4.5 mA to 19.5 mA either way at 25 °C. With all supplies at 0 V, an output still shows at least 300 Ω between -2 V and 2 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Receiver inputs",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "A receiver input withstands ±30 V. The receiver inverts: its output goes low when the input rises past VIT+ (1.9 V typ) and high again when the input falls below VIT- (0.97 V typ), with at least 0.5 V of hysteresis. An open input gives a high output, at least 2.6 V. The input draws 3.6 mA to 8.3 mA at ±25 V and at least 0.43 mA at ±3 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "All logic-side pins (RY, DA, VCC, GND) are on pins 11 to 20 and all line-side pins (RA, DY, VDD, VSS) on pins 1 to 10, with each channel's input opposite its output, so the board routes from the UART to the connector without crossovers."
  }
 ],
